00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:04.380
That rotation idea pairs naturally with understanding how Canadian dividends are actually taxed.

00:05:04.620 --> 00:05:09.740
Imagine you bought a Canadian bank stock inside your TFSA last quarter,

00:05:10.240 --> 00:05:13.360
expecting the dividend to compound at a 4% yield.

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:21.420
Your brokerage shows the cash hitting the account, but here is what actually happens between the declaration date and the deposit.

00:05:22.420 --> 00:05:29.780
The eligible dividend receives the gross up and dividend tax credit treatment that only applies to Canadian corporations,

00:05:30.060 --> 00:05:38.640
turning a 4% headline yield into an after-tax equivalent closer to 5.3% for someone in the 40% marginal bracket.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:45.940
The mechanism works because the dividend tax credit offsets the gross up at both federal and provincial levels,

00:05:45.940 --> 00:05:50.820
a feature unavailable to US dividends or REIT distributions.

00:05:51.480 --> 00:06:00.060
What most retail investors do not realize is that the credit is only available on eligible dividends from Canadian companies

00:06:00.060 --> 00:06:08.720
that pay tax at the full corporate rate, so checking the issuer's tax status on Cedar Plus before purchase avoids surprises.

00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:17.200
The professionals always verify free cash flow coverage above 1.2 times the dividend before committing capital,

00:06:17.860 --> 00:06:21.460
rather than chasing headline yields above 7%.

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:29.320
The biggest mistake with Canadian dividend investing is loading up on high yield names without confirming payout sustainability.

00:06:30.220 --> 00:06:37.880
Instead, always screen for dividend aristocrats on the TSX that have raised payouts for 10 or more consecutive years,

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:44.660
and keep the position size under 8% of the TFS-A to maintain diversification.

